# Development guide

## Quickstart

```bash
gh repo clone {{ github_owner }}/{{ project_name }}
cd {{ project_name }}

# Install uv
curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh

# Install dependencies. uv reads .python-version to pick the interpreter for .venv.
uv sync --all-groups --all-extras

# Install pre-commit hooks if you plan to modify the code. GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM hides a
# system-level core.hooksPath, which pre-commit refuses to install over.
GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM=1 uv run pre-commit install
```

## Creating commits and pull requests

### Branch naming

Use the pattern `username/two-four-word-description`, for example `{{ github_owner }}/add-rate-limiting`.

### Commit messages

Follow Conventional Commits (`conventionalcommits.org`):

```txt
<type>(scope): <subject>
```

The `conventional-commit-subject` hook in `.pre-commit-config.yaml` checks the type and the
72-character limit, and its regex lists the types it accepts. Write the subject in imperative
mood ("add", "fix", "remove", not "added", "fixes", "removed"). Keep the whole message under
100 words.

```txt
feat(api): add rate limiting
fix(worker): retry on transient timeouts
docs(readme): update local setup instructions
```

### Pre-commit hooks

`GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM=1 uv run pre-commit install` registers the hooks in `.pre-commit-config.yaml`. CI runs the same
hooks and enforces changed-line coverage (below).

`GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM=1` is there because `pre-commit install` refuses to run when a
system-level `core.hooksPath` is set, which managed macOS fleets use for a mandatory secret
scanner. It applies to the install only. Commits still read that system setting, so the hook
this writes to `.git/hooks/pre-commit` runs only if the system hook chains to it, which those
scanners normally do. Confirm it once:

```bash
git config --get core.hooksPath          # empty: nothing to worry about
git hook run --ignore-missing pre-commit # your hooks should run; silence means unreachable
```

If they do not run, ask whoever owns the system hook to chain to `.git/hooks`. Do not point
`core.hooksPath` at `.git/hooks` yourself: that disables the fleet's scanner for this
repository.

Some hooks (ruff) modify files in place. The commit then aborts because the working tree changed
after staging; re-add and commit again:

```bash
git add -u
git commit
```

Do not skip hooks with `--no-verify`. If a hook fails and you do not understand why, ask before
bypassing it.

### Pull requests

Follow `.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md`. One logical change per PR; at roughly 300 changed
lines, split it.

## Running the tests

Test files live next to the code they cover and end with `*_test.py`, never `test_*.py`.

`addopts` in `pyproject.toml` skips live tests, so `just test` and a bare `uv run pytest` run
the unit and integration tests. `just cov` skips the integration tests as well and adds coverage,
then checks the changed lines. Pre-commit and CI run that same coverage command, and fail a change
that covers under 80% of its own changed lines. Reach the live tests with `-m live`.

## Toolchain

Runtime dependencies live under `[project.dependencies]` in `pyproject.toml`, development tools
under `[dependency-groups.dev]`. `.pre-commit-config.yaml` shows which of those tools run on a
commit. Three type checkers run there -- mypy, basedpyright, and pyrefly -- because each catches
what the others miss.
